  "query_preview": "..The upper pattern this morning is defined by a\nstrong ridge of high pressure centered over Louisiana and a\nbroadening trough of low pressure along the West Coast. The\nlatest GOES precipitable water imagery indicated a much drier \nairmass has positioned itself into Southeast Arizona, with PWATs\naround an inch across Cochise county and around 1.25 inches \nacross the Tucson Metro and the lower deserts of Pima and Pinal \ncounties. Lower-level moisture still hanging-on a bit more than \nexpected primarily due to the recently saturated soils, with \nsurface dewpoints generally in the low to mid 60s. The exception\nto the rule is across Graham and Greenlee counties with \ndewpoints in the lower 50s. Although the will be a lack of \nlarger scale lift across the area, there may be enough moisture \nfor a slight chance of afternoon showers and thunderstorms. \nThere wont be much threat for widespread heavy rain today, but \nwith the drier mid-levels across the area, some of the storms to\ndo develop will be capable of producing localized gusty and \nerratic winds to 40 mph.\n\nThe upper pattern over the next few days will become more \nstable as the 500mb upper-high center slowly moves overhead by \nSunday and the 300mb high develops over the northern Baja. Weak \ninverted trough near extreme NE Mexico will rotate toward our \nneck of the woods this weekend, impacting the far SE portions of\nAZ Sunday afternoon into Monday afternoon. Not too much \nmoisture around to work with, but we may see more thunderstorms \nactivity starting Sunday across Cochise county and along the \nInternational Border.\n\nThe more impactful weather element in this forecast package will\ninvolve the heat next week. With high pressure aloft, we will \nsee thicknesses increase enough to put portions of Southeast \nArizona in the major HeatRisk category with isolated pockets of \nextreme. An Extreme Heat Watch in currently in effect from \nlate Monday morning through Wednesday evening. Although models\nare still bullish with keeping the high center nearby, models\nmove the high center slightly north which will open Southeast \nArizona up to a more favorable easterly flow for thunderstorm \nactivity. Not only will this pattern result in a tad more \nlower-level moisture to advect into the area from the east but \nthe possibility of more cloud cover may moderate surface heating\nby a degree or two the second half of next week. For this \nreason, we have decided to hold off on extending the Extreme \nHeat Watch into next Thursday and Friday.",
